{
  "requirement": "Each language's fingerprint rules determine which attachments\ncontribute to the owning item's structural hash.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** `requirements.sdk.outlines.attachments.fingerprint` says each language's fingerprint rules decide which attachments contribute to the owning item's structural hash. Each bundled language's `fingerprints` requirement and its attachments section state those rules. I derived the expected outcome for each language from them:\n\n- **Rust:** doc comments and attributes contribute. Ordinary comments are excluded.\n- **Python:** decorators contribute, and so do docstrings, which are ordinary expressions. `#` comments are excluded.\n- **TypeScript and TSX:** all comments, including `/** */`, are excluded.\n- **CSS, TOML and YAML:** comments are excluded.\n- **Markdown:** HTML comment blocks are excluded. Inline HTML comments stay paragraph text and contribute.\n- **Plain text:** it has no attachments.\n\n**Test mapping.**\n\n- **Rust**, `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/tests.rs#fn[hashes_skip_whitespace_and_plain_comments_but_not_doc_comments]`:\n  - Changing the `// {cite}` ordinary comment preserves the hash, including when the item is re-laid out and moved.\n  - Changing the `/// Doc.` text changes the hash.\n  - Changing the `#[inline]` attribute changes the hash.\n  - Changing the body changes the hash.\n  - A parent module's structural hash changes while its identity hash does not.\n- **Python**, `...#fn[python_decorators_attach_to_the_decorated_definition_for_hashing]`:\n  - Changing a decorator, or removing it, changes the hash.\n  - Blank lines and indentation changes do not.\n  - A method decorator changes the class's structural hash but not its identity hash.\n- **All seven bundled languages**, `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[bundled_fingerprints_exclude_comments_and_preserve_word_boundaries]` covers Rust, Python, TS, TSX, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML:\n  - Changing an ordinary comment, or a TS/TSX `/** */` doc comment, leaves the fingerprint unchanged. The Rust case uses `/// two words` against `/// two   words`, and the Rust doc comment stays included.\n  - Joining words changes the fingerprint.\n  - The case also asserts a 64-hex fingerprint.\n  - A Python docstring contributes: changed words change the hash, and reflowed whitespace does not.\n  - An inline Markdown HTML comment contributes: changing its text changes the hash.\n  - The case exercises the Rust block comment `/* note */` and the TOML and YAML trailing-position comments inside the item.\n\n**Rest of the Rust rules.** The remaining Rust inclusion rules are covered, though not under this requirement. These are inner `//!` comments, block doc comments, and inner and outer grouping. The tests that cover them are marked for `requirements.sdk.sources.rust.fingerprints`. They are `reflowing_doc_comments_keeps_the_hash`, `reflowing_inner_and_block_doc_comments_keeps_the_hash` and `inner_and_outer_doc_comments_hash_in_separate_groups`. They use the same per-language comment-hashing decision, which the marked tests already exercise. I treat this as no adequacy gap. Those tests' obligations belong to the per-language fingerprint requirements.\n\n**Gaps.** I found no unmarked or untested attachment-inclusion or exclusion case for any bundled language, and no missing assertions. There are no acknowledgments and none are needed.",
  "references": "2272dba8cdd32c9845b99dc74f77952eb96d2f2046ea64ab9502ddab77190019",
